            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous coronary dissection resulting in acute myocardial infarction: successful treatment with primary angioplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>62-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spontaneous coronary dissection is an unusual cause of acute myocardial infarction for which the optimal therapy has not been established. We report the case of a 63-year-old man with acute lateral wall myocardial infarction resulting from spontaneous dissection of the major diagonal branch of the left anterior descending artery in whom acute and long-term patency was restored by primary PTCA.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
